rker", "x": 41, "y": 35, "facing": "left", "move": "still", "trainer": "tci_salt_t"}, {"id": "tci_heckler", "sprite": "festival_goer", "x": 29, "y": 30, "facing": "up", "move": "still", "trainer": "tci_heckler"}, {"id": "tci_bird_t", "sprite": "birdwatcher", "x": 40, "y": 20, "facing": "down", "move": "still", "trainer": "tci_bird_t"}, {"id": "tci_diver", "sprite": "surfer", "x": 24, "y": 37, "facing": "right", "move": "still", "trainer": "tci_diver"}, {"id": "tci_wreck", "sprite": "sailor", "x": 32, "y": 45, "facing": "up", "move": "still", "trainer": "tci_wreck"}, {"id": "turks_caicos_bureau", "sprite": "commuter", "x": 30, "y": 24, "facing": "down", "move": "still", "script": "turks_caicos_bureau", "name": "Bureau Clerk"}, {"id": "turks_caicos_homebound", "sprite": "sailor", "x": 31, "y": 24, "facing": "down", "move": "still", "script": "turks_caicos_homebound", "name": "Movements Clerk"}], "encounters": {"grass": [{"species": "flamingale", "min": 95, "max": 97, "weight": 10}, {"species": "conchling", "min": 95, "max": 97, "weight": 14}, {"species": "duneskink", "min": 95, "max": 97, "weight": 14, "weather": ["clear", "sunshine"]}, {"species": "chipgull", "min": 95, "max": 97, "weight": 14}, {"species": "ternlet", "min": 95, "max": 97, "weight": 10}, {"species": "pinchkin", "min": 95, "max": 97, "weight": 12}, {"species": "gannetling", "min": 95, "max": 97, "weight": 10}, {"species": "plungebeak", "min": 95, "max": 97, "weight": 10, "time": ["dawn", "dusk"]}, {"species": "moolet", "min": 95, "max": 97, "weight": 6}, {"species": "croakle", "min": 95, "max": 97, "weight": 8}, {"species": "swiftlet", "min": 95, "max": 97, "weight": 8, "weather": ["gale", "clear"]}, {"species": "wideling", "min": 95, "max": 97, "weight": 6, "weather": ["clear", "sunshine"]}], "water": [{"species": "conchling", "min": 95, "max": 97, "weight": 28}, {"species": "dolphlet", "min": 95, "max": 97, "weight": 20}, {"species": "baskling", "min": 95, "max": 97, "weight": 16}, {"species": "plungebeak", "min": 95, "max": 97, "weight": 14}, {"species": "swelklet", "min": 95, "max": 97, "weight": 14}, {"species": "conchord", "min": 96, "max": 98, "weight": 6}]}, "signs": [{"x": 31, "y": 25, "text": "COCKBURN TOWN, GRAND TURK. Colonial lanes, salt-white walls and more donkeys than traffic lights. Pedestrians keep left, donkeys keep right."}, {"x": 33, "y": 17, "text": "THE LIGHTHOUSE, north. Climb, look, take a photograph, tell no one how many steps."}, {"x": 39, "y": 17, "text": "SALT PONDS. Keep clear of the pink water. Flamingos are watching. They are always watching."}, {"x": 33, "y": 39, "text": "THE AIRFIELD, south. And, round the corner, a spaceship."}, {"x": 22, "y": 20, "text": "THE NAME. The Turks Islands are named for the Turk's head cactus, which looks like a fez. The Caicos Islands probably take their name from a Lucayan phrase meaning a string of islands. A fez and a necklace: it is not the worst way to name a country.", "plaque": "tci_name", "title": "The Name"}, {"x": 37, "y": 33, "text": "WHITE GOLD. Salt made these islands. Sea water was let into shallow ponds, baked by the sun and raked into heaps. By the 1700s salt from Grand Turk and Salt Cay was shipped all over the Atlantic, and the raking went on for more than two hundred years.", "plaque": "tci_salt", "title": "White Gold"}, {"x": 25, "y": 36, "text": "THE BERMUDIAN RAKERS. From the 1600s, Bermudian sailors came to the Turks to rake salt each season, and Bermudians were the main salt rakers for generations. Many of the older houses in Cockburn Town look very like Bermuda's, with good reason.", "plaque": "tci_bermuda", "title": "The Bermudian Rakers"}, {"x": 30, "y": 22, "text": "MOLASSES REEF WRECK. Off the Caicos Bank lies the Molasses Reef wreck, dated to about 1513 to 1520, the oldest European shipwreck yet excavated in the Americas. Many of its finds are in the National Museum in Cockburn Town.", "plaque": "tci_molasses", "title": "Molasses Reef Wreck"}, {"x": 35, "y": 45, "text": "FRIENDSHIP 7. On 20 February 1962 the astronaut John Glenn splashed down in the Atlantic near Grand Turk after becoming the first American to orbit the Earth, and was brought ashore here. A replica of his Friendship 7 capsule stands near the airport.", "plaque": "tci_glenn", "title": "Friendship 7"}, {"x": 36, "y": 9, "text": "GRAND TURK LIGHTHOUSE. Built in 1852 from cast-iron parts shipped out from England, to end the wrecks on the reef off the north of the island. It still stands on the cliff and is one of the oldest lighthouses in the region.", "plaque": "tci_lighthouse", "title": "Grand Turk Lighthouse"}, {"x": 37, "y": 17, "text": "THE FLAMINGOS. Caribbean flamingos feed in the shallow salt ponds, where brine shrimp give them their pink. They stand on one leg to save heat, and they all face the same way, a habit that makes any flock look like a rehearsal.", "plaque": "tci_flamingo", "title": "The Flamingos"}, {"x": 25, "y": 27, "text": "QUEEN CONCH. The queen conch is a large sea snail with a pink-lined shell, and a staple of Turks and Caicos cooking: raw in salad, fried in fritters or in stew. Overfishing has hit conch across the Caribbean, and the islands now have a closed season and a conch farm.", "plaque": "tci_conch", "title": "Queen Conch"}, {"x": 29, "y": 27, "text": "RIPSAW MUSIC. Ripsaw, or rake-and-scrape, is the national music: a carpenter's saw scraped with a screwdriver to keep rhythm, with a goatskin drum, a concertina and a guitar or two. It makes more noise than any other saw in the world.", "plaque": "tci_ripsaw", "title": "Ripsaw Music"}, {"x": 42, "y": 28, "text": "CONCH BAR CAVES. Middle Caicos is home to Conch Bar Caves, the largest above-water cave system in the Bahamian chain of islands. Bats live in them, and the Lucayan people used them long before Columbus passed by.", "plaque": "tci_cave", "title": "Conch Bar Caves"}, {"x": 22, "y": 27, "text": "COLUMBUS AND THE LUCAYANS. The Lucayans lived here before Europeans arrived, and some historians argue that Columbus's first landfall in 1492 was on Grand Turk. Others argue he landed in the Bahamas. Both camps will happily argue about it with you for an afternoon.", "plaque": "tci_columbus", "title": "Columbus and the Lucayans"}, {"x": 38, "y": 26, "text": "GRACE BAY. On Providenciales, thirty minutes by air from here, Grace Bay beach is regularly voted among the best in the world. It is long, white and the colour of a swimming pool in a holiday advert, and it has drawn most of the tourists in the islands.", "plaque": "tci_grace_bay", "title": "Grace Bay"}], "legend_extra": {"_water": {"deep": "#0a5f8a", "deep_top": "#3cc0d2", "shallow": "#52dcd0"}, "$": {"tile": "sea", "colour": "#12829f"}, "~": {"tile": "water", "colour": "#33b4c0"}, "w": {"tile": "shallow", "colour": "#86e4d6"}, ".": {"tile": "grass", "colour": "#a2b14c"}, ",": {"tile": "tall_grass", "colour": "#8a9c40", "prop": "short"}, "s": {"tile": "sand", "colour": "#f6eed6"}, "p": {"tile": "sand", "colour": "#f8f2df"}, "t": {"tile": "tree_oak", "prop": "palm"}, "e": {"tile": "tree_oak", "prop": "palm"}, "r": {"tile": "rock", "colour": "#b8b09a"}, "b": {"tile": "shallow", "colour": "#e8a9bd"}, "n": {"tile": "sand", "colour": "#f4f6f4"}, "c": {"tile": "tall_grass", "colour": "#7f9a3a", "prop": "short"}, "#": {"tile": "path", "colour": "#eadcbc"}, "=": {"tile": "road", "colour": "#c4bca6"}, ":": {"tile": "cobbles", "colour": "#dccfb4"}, "f": {"tile": "flowers", "colour": "#a2b14c"}}, "rows": ["$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$", "$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$", "$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$", "$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$", "$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$", "$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$", "$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$w$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$